From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 08:53:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wintrust: Keep a signer's time stamps as counter signers, and
 verify it as of the first that holds.

A legacy counter signature and an RFC 3161 time stamp token are both
kept as counter signers of type SGNR_TYPE_TIMESTAMP, each verified (the
legacy one with CryptMsgVerifyCountersignatureEncodedEx, the token with
CryptVerifyTimeStampSignature) and with its own chain, built as of its
time for time stamping from the message's and the token's certificates.
A signer is verified as of the first time stamp whose signature and
chain hold, else as of the call.  RFC 3161 time stamps were not read at
all, so a signature whose certificate has since expired, which is most
of Office's, was verified as of now; and the legacy time was taken
unverified.  On Windows 11 build 29671, measured with
tools/wintrustprobe on files of Office and OfficePLUS, each time stamp is
a counter signer of type 0x10 with a chain of three, and the signer is
verified as of its time to the millisecond.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

